:question
$()	<div style="font-family: verdana,  sans-serif ;  font-size: 11pt ; background-color: yellow ;">	<p> 	Let 
!insmath f
$() be the function  defined over 
!insmath $val28
$() by:	
!insmath f(x) = $val19
$().	</p><p>	So we have 
!insmath f = $val20 
$()  where, for any real 
!insmath x
$() in 
!insmath $val28
$(), 	
!insmath u(x) = $val12
$()  &nbsp; and  &nbsp; 
!insmath v(x) = $val13
$().	</p><p> 	The aim of the exercise is to evaluate step by step the limit of 
!insmath f
$() at $val26.	</p>	<small><b> 
!read oef/special.phtml help saisie,Typing hints 
$()</b></small>	</div>		<ul>				 
!if $m_step=1 
$()<li>Evaluate the limit of 
!insmath v
$() at $val26 : 	<center>
!insmath \lim_{x\to$val27} $val13 
$() =  
!read oef/embed.phtml reply1,5 
$()</center>	</li> 
!endif
$()	 
!if $m_step>1 
$()<li><font color = blue> The limit of  
!insmath v
$() at $val26 is</font>:  	<center>
!insmath \lim_{x\to$val27} v(x) = \lim_{x\to$val27} $val13  = $val31
$()</center>	</li>	 
!endif
$()			 
!if $m_step=2 
$()<li>Evaluate the limit of  
!insmath u
$() at $val26 : 	<center>
!insmath \lim_{x\to$val27} $val12 
$() =  
!read oef/embed.phtml reply2,5 
$()</center>	</li>	 
!endif
$()	 
!if $m_step>2 
$()<li><font color = blue> The limit of   
!insmath u
$() at $val26  is</font>:  	<center>
!insmath \lim_{x\to$val27} u(x) = \lim_{x\to$val27} $val12  = $val34
$()</center>	</li>	 
!endif
$()			 
!if $m_step=3 
$()<li>Evaluate the limit of 
!insmath $val15
$() at 
!insmath $val34
$() : 	<center>
!insmath \lim_{t\to$val34} $val15 
$() =  
!read oef/embed.phtml reply3,5 
$()</center>	</li>	 
!endif
$()			 
!if $m_step=4 
$()<li>By variable renaming 
!insmath t = $val12
$(), knowing that
!insmath \lim_{t\to$val34} $val15  = $val37
$(), it comes that: 	<center>
!insmath  \lim_{x\to$val27} $val18 
$() =  
!read oef/embed.phtml reply4,5 
$()</center>	</li> 	 
!endif
$()	 
!if $m_step>4 
$()<li>	<font color = blue>The limit of 
!insmath $val16
$() at $val26 is</font>:	<center>
!insmath \lim_{x\to$val27} $val17 =\lim_{x\to$val27} $val18  = \lim_{t\to$val34} $val15 = $val39
$(). </center> 	</li>	 
!endif
$()		 
!if $m_step=5 
$()<li> Can we deduce the limit at $val26  of 
!insmath f = $val20
$() by applying the computational rules of limits ? 	<center> 
!read oef/embed.phtml reply5,size=5 
$()</center> 	</li>	 
!endif
$()		 
!if $m_step=6 
$()<li><font color=blue>	 
!if $val44=1 
$()	The computational rules of limits are valid, because there is no indeterminate form.</font>  
!else
$()	 The computational rules of limits are <b>not</b> valid, because there is an indeterminate form $val45 .	</font> <br/> We use instead the properties of "compared growth":	<i> 
!if $val14=1 
$()the exponential function dominates any polynom function 
!else
$() any polynom function dominates the logarithm function 
!endif
$().</i>	. 
!endif
$()	<br/>	Then it comes:	<center>
!insmath \lim_{x\to$val27} f(x)
$() =  
!read oef/embed.phtml reply6,5 
$() </center>	</li> 
!endif
$() 		</ul>	
$()
